From 7625fabebc03dbf3479917213b340f9842dedb18 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Neil Johnson <neil.johnson71@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 17:58:41 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 20/26] asrab: "add sp,n" takes a signed displacement, so do
 not range check it

The Rabbit's ADD SP,n adds an eight bit displacement to SP.  It is
signed in use - negative to allocate a stack frame, positive to free
one - and a compiler emits far more of the first.

rabmch.c emitted the operand with outrb(&e2, R_USGN), so "add sp,#-6"
came back as "<v> Unsigned Number Exceeded Range" with the correct
bytes already in the listing: the check does not change the encoding,
it only refuses to let it out.  Measured against SDCC's library, that
is 190 sites in each of the six Rabbit ports, every one of them a
stack frame being allocated.

Both signs name the same byte and both are meant, so there is no range
to check and the flag is dropped rather than swapped for R_SGND -
which would refuse "add sp,#200", freeing a frame larger than 127.
sdas reached the same conclusion and says so in a comment,
"n=signed displacement".

astest/cases/rabsp pins each negative value assembling to the same
byte as the unsigned spelling of that byte, which is the argument.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 asrab/rabmch.c | 9 ++++++++-
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/asrab/rabmch.c b/asrab/rabmch.c
index fa8f2be..56af0f2 100644
--- a/asrab/rabmch.c
+++ b/asrab/rabmch.c
@@ -945,12 +945,19 @@ machine(struct mne *mp)
 		}
                 /*
                  * add  sp,n	[sp,#n]
+                 *
+                 * n is a signed displacement: negative to
+                 * allocate a stack frame, positive to free one.
+                 * Checking it as unsigned refused every frame
+                 * allocation, which is the common case, so the
+                 * byte is emitted without a range check - both
+                 * signs name the same byte and both are meant.
                  */
 		if ((mchtyp == X_R2K) && (rf == S_ADD) &&
 		    (t1 == S_R16) && (e1.e_addr == SP) &&
 		    (t2 == S_IMMED)) {
 			outab(0x27);
-			outrb(&e2, R_USGN);
+			outrb(&e2, 0);
 			break;
                 }
 		xerr('a', "Invalid Addressing Mode.");
